1
00:00:00,050 --> 00:00:02,088
Thursday, eleven at night.

2
00:00:02,138 --> 00:00:07,560
The bank's payments are quiet, and Timur is about to upgrade the card zone.

3
00:00:07,610 --> 00:00:11,856
Tomorrow is Friday, and Malika's toy shop will be busy.

4
00:00:11,906 --> 00:00:16,968
He wants to know exactly what will run in the morning, and exactly how to go back.

5
00:00:17,018 --> 00:00:23,448
In Lesson 39, the same install ran on a laptop, a test server and production.

6
00:00:23,498 --> 00:00:29,184
Only the values changed, so the product itself must be exactly the same too.

7
00:00:29,234 --> 00:00:34,512
This lesson numbers each release, so everyone knows exactly what is running.

8
00:00:34,562 --> 00:00:38,808
Imagine the card zone always ran whatever is newest.

9
00:00:38,858 --> 00:00:44,232
On Tuesday, a rebuild quietly pulls a newer version nobody tested.

10
00:00:44,282 --> 00:00:50,712
On Wednesday, someone rolls back, pulls the newest again, and gets the same broken build.

11
00:00:50,762 --> 00:00:57,000
Then the bank's assessor asks: what exactly runs in your card zone, and what is inside it?

12
00:00:57,050 --> 00:00:58,872
Nobody can answer.

13
00:00:58,922 --> 00:01:02,256
A moving name can never say what runs.

14
00:01:02,306 --> 00:01:07,200
I think newest is the most dangerous word you can write in an install.

15
00:01:07,250 --> 00:01:12,560
So what should an install say, instead of newest?

16
00:01:12,610 --> 00:01:16,904
So each part of the system numbers its versions in three parts.

17
00:01:16,954 --> 00:01:21,536
The first number grows when a change breaks something callers rely on.

18
00:01:21,586 --> 00:01:24,200
The second grows when something is added.

19
00:01:24,250 --> 00:01:26,504
The third grows for a fix.

20
00:01:26,554 --> 00:01:29,792
So the number alone tells you how careful to be.

21
00:01:29,842 --> 00:01:37,448
A release is one numbered version, made once, from one exact state of the code, and never changed after.

22
00:01:37,498 --> 00:01:41,600
Everything is built from it, and published under its number.

23
00:01:41,650 --> 00:01:49,184
Each built piece carries a bill of materials: a list of every component inside it, each with its version.

24
00:01:49,234 --> 00:01:52,040
It also carries a record of how it was built.

25
00:01:52,090 --> 00:01:57,008
So the assessor's question has an answer, written on the piece itself.

26
00:01:57,058 --> 00:01:59,648
Every install pins its versions.

27
00:01:59,698 --> 00:02:04,616
A pin is one line that names the exact release a product runs.

28
00:02:04,666 --> 00:02:10,544
There is one line for the card zone, one for ecom, and one for the portals.

29
00:02:10,594 --> 00:02:14,984
Every environment runs real releases, the laptop too.

30
00:02:15,034 --> 00:02:18,968
So what Bekzod tests is exactly what the bank runs.

31
00:02:19,018 --> 00:02:21,704
An upgrade is four steps.

32
00:02:21,754 --> 00:02:23,576
Back up.

33
00:02:23,626 --> 00:02:25,448
Change the pinned line.

34
00:02:25,498 --> 00:02:27,320
Deploy.

35
00:02:27,370 --> 00:02:29,192
Check.

36
00:02:29,242 --> 00:02:33,800
The card zone goes first, before ecom, which depends on it.

37
00:02:33,850 --> 00:02:40,328
The release notes are read before starting: they say what changed, and whether a new value is needed.

38
00:02:40,378 --> 00:02:43,256
And the database only moves forward.

39
00:02:43,306 --> 00:02:48,128
A release can change the shape of the database, but never steps it back.

40
00:02:48,178 --> 00:02:54,392
So rolling back across such a change means restoring the backup taken just before.

41
00:02:54,442 --> 00:02:57,320
That is why the backup is always step one.

42
00:02:57,370 --> 00:03:00,176
Timur opens the list of releases.

43
00:03:00,226 --> 00:03:02,768
Each has a plain number and its notes.

44
00:03:02,818 --> 00:03:06,488
He chooses the newest, and reads its notes first.

45
00:03:06,538 --> 00:03:13,088
They say whether a breaking change is inside, and whether the installer will ask for a new value.

46
00:03:13,138 --> 00:03:15,632
On the server, he takes the backup.

47
00:03:15,682 --> 00:03:23,744
Then he changes one line: the card zone's pinned release, from one point three point two to one point four point zero.

48
00:03:23,794 --> 00:03:25,616
He deploys.

49
00:03:25,666 --> 00:03:29,600
The services restart on the new release, in order.

50
00:03:29,650 --> 00:03:32,888
The health check runs: every service healthy.

51
00:03:32,938 --> 00:03:35,744
Only then does he do the same for ecom.

52
00:03:35,794 --> 00:03:41,048
A month later, the assessor asks again: what is inside the card zone?

53
00:03:41,098 --> 00:03:45,368
Timur opens the bill of materials of one of its pieces.

54
00:03:45,418 --> 00:03:51,776
It lists every component inside, each with its version, and the count sits at the bottom.

55
00:03:51,826 --> 00:03:56,408
That is the answer, and the assessor can trust every piece.

56
00:03:56,458 --> 00:04:02,216
Friday night, Malika's orders run on exactly the release Timur chose.

57
00:04:02,266 --> 00:04:05,912
Nothing changes unless someone changes a pinned line.

58
00:04:05,962 --> 00:04:13,016
If he must go back, the old release is still exactly what it was, and the backup came before the change.

59
00:04:13,066 --> 00:04:18,512
To recap: every release has one number, and every install pins it.

60
00:04:18,562 --> 00:04:23,152
So how does Timur go back, if he must?

61
00:04:23,202 --> 00:04:28,168
He pins the old release, and restores the backup taken before.

62
00:04:28,218 --> 00:04:31,072
Next: who is responsible for what.
